  "worked_solution": "First, we define the function $h(x)$ as\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $h(x)>0.$\n\nThe equation $h(x)=0$ has no solution because ${x}^{2}+1>0$ for all real $x.$ On the other hand, $h(x)$ has a single vertical asymptote at $x=3.$ This asymptote divides the number line into $2$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $h(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $h(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $h(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $h(x)$ is positive on the interval $(3,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\nh(x)>0\\text{\u00a0for all\u00a0}x\\in (\\,3,\\infty \\,).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
